  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "## 12.4.2\n\n### Behavioural changes\n- The text formatting set on a `FlowDocument` (`FontFamily`, `FontSize`, `FontWeight`, `FontStyle`, `FontStretch`, `Foreground`, `Background`) is the document's default text formatting in the model (`TextDocument.DefaultTextFormatting`), so every snapshot carries it on its root and serializers see it. It lived on the facade only, so a document's font never reached a saved file. A `FlowDocument` over a loaded or model-first `TextDocument` presents the model's values, so a document read with a default font shows in it. A value the document only inherits from the control hosting it is not the document's and stays out. Setting `TextDocument.DefaultTextFormatting` while a `FlowDocument` presents the document replaces the facade's values, a value set on the `FlowDocument` earlier among them, so a view showing the document re-renders in the new defaults.\n\n### Fixes\n- Character formatting passes a preformatted block by, as a paste into one already arrives as plain text. A Markdown code block took bold, a font size or any other character formatting applied to a range across it or at a caret inside it, and lost it on saving, since a fenced code block cannot carry it. Applying or toggling a character property on a range skips such blocks, and at a caret inside one it neither formats nor springloads. This holds for every block kind registered with `NodeKindFlags.Preformatted`.\n- A paste with the caret in an empty table cell or an empty list item goes into that cell or item. A table, list or section without any text was taken for an empty line: when it was the document's only block the paste replaced it, and otherwise the pasted content landed above it. An empty preformatted block takes the paste as its own text the same way.\n- A paragraph's first-line indent (`Block.TextIndent`, read from RTF `\\fi`, DOCX `w:firstLine`/`w:hanging` and XAML) is laid out: the first line starts that far from the paragraph edge, a negative value hangs it out to the left, and the lines after a wrap or a line break start at the edge. The indent was kept in the document and written back out but the layout always used 0. Drawing it needs an Avalonia version whose text formatter applies `TextParagraphProperties.Indent` (AvaloniaUI/Avalonia#22870); on earlier versions the paragraph lays out as before.\n- The text after an embedded object that nothing draws is laid out. A `RichInlineUIContainer` without a child ended its paragraph's layout, so the rest of the paragraph never showed, though it stayed in the document. Such an object now keeps its place in the line and paints nothing.",
